  "worked_solution": "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+49{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+49=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation has pure imaginary roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+49 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-49 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 7\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=P\\operatorname{cos}\u20617x+Q\\operatorname{sin}\u20617x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=7.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{\u2032}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-7P\\operatorname{sin}\u20617x+7Q\\operatorname{cos}\u20617x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=7$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n7Q=7.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-2$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2\\operatorname{cos}\u20617x+\\operatorname{sin}\u20617x.\n$$",
